---
/**
 * The hero's two instruments as they look: the player's box, the ledger's
 * panel, the no-JavaScript fallback, and the `--playdeck-*` values the
 * library's optional theme reads.
 *
 * The split with `HeroPlayerIsland.tsx` is deliberate and is the same split the
 * library itself draws. That file composes primitives, points them at a source
 * and reads what they report; this one decides what the result looks like.
 * Nothing about the composition is expressible here and nothing about the
 * appearance is expressible there, so neither file can quietly grow the other's
 * job.
 *
 * ---- why every selector below is `:global()` --------------------------------
 *
 * Astro scopes a component's rules by stamping an attribute onto the elements
 * in its own template, and the elements these rules address are not in it: both
 * panels are rendered by React inside the island, which no scope attribute
 * reaches. So the choice is not between scoped and global rules, it is between
 * global rules and rules that match nothing.
 *
 * `:global()` per selector rather than `<style is:global>` for the whole block:
 * this file already had to write it that way for the library's own parts, so
 * the file keeps one spelling throughout, and each rule states at its own head
 * that it is addressing markup produced elsewhere. The class names are this
 * page's and appear nowhere else on the site, which is what makes that safe.
 *
 * ---- why `client:only` and not a hydrating directive ------------------------
 *
 * `client:visible` and its siblings render the component on the server and then
 * hydrate it. Both halves of that are wrong here. The server-rendered half
 * would put a real `<button>` into the document that does nothing until a
 * script arrives, which is the "present and disabled" shape this whole site
 * argues against — and for a reader with JavaScript off it would never stop
 * being that. The hydrating half then has to reproduce, in a browser holding a
 * media element, markup that was produced where there was none to measure; any
 * disagreement between the two is a console error on the site's root document,
 * and console errors on that document are one of the things
 * `scripts/check-deploy-artifact.mjs` fails the deployed artifact on. That is a
 * risk this page has no reason to take, not a defect anyone has reproduced.
 *
 * `client:only` renders nothing on the server, so neither problem exists. What
 * it costs is a hero row that is empty until the island mounts, and nothing
 * reserves that row's height today — the page below it moves down when the two
 * panels arrive. Stated rather than left to be discovered: it is a real cost of
 * the directive above, and the paragraph that used to claim `index.astro`
 * reserved the height was describing a stage this file no longer renders.
 *
 * ---- the two theme systems, and why they do not meet ------------------------
 *
 * `@playdeck/react/theme.css` is imported below. It is the player's theme, not
 * this site's, and `DESIGN.md` puts it out of this system's scope — so the
 * import needs its reason stated rather than assumed.
 *
 * The reason is that the Headless section of this page tells a reader the
 * primitives import no CSS and that the default look is one optional import.
 * The hero is where that sentence is either demonstrated or merely asserted,
 * and demonstrating it costs one import rather than a second stylesheet of this
 * site's own restating a control bar the library already ships and tests.
 *
 * They cannot bleed into each other, and that is a property of the file rather
 * than of this page's care. Every selector in it is inside `@layer playdeck`
 * and wrapped in `:where()`, so it matches only elements carrying a
 * `data-playdeck-part` attribute — which nothing on this site has except inside
 * the island — and loses to any unlayered rule here regardless of specificity.
 * In the other direction it declares no token of its own: every value is read
 * as `var(--playdeck-…, fallback)`, so the block below is the whole of what
 * this site says to it. That is why the mapping is a list of custom properties
 * on one element and not a set of overriding rules.
 *
 * The mapping spends this site's role tokens and writes no colour, so rule 1 of
 * `DESIGN.md` holds across the seam: the player re-tunes with the theme switch
 * because it is reading the same roles every other panel on the page reads.
 */
import '@playdeck/react/theme.css';
import HeroPlayerIsland from './HeroPlayerIsland';

// Resolved against the configured base the way every other address on this site
// is, rather than written as a root-absolute literal. The file is this app's
// own — `public/tracer.mp4`, copied into this build rather than referenced
// across an app boundary, because an Astro build serves only its own `public/`.
const clipUrl = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}tracer.mp4`;
// The same fixture the archetypes caption their clip with (#542 phase 2), so
// CH 01's captions and settings-menu stages have a real track to build from.
const captionsUrl = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}archetype-captions.vtt`;
---

{
  /* The island, which renders the hero's own panel (the player) and, by
   * portal, the ledger that reads it further down the page in the `truth`
   * section. An `<astro-island>` is `display: contents`, so the player lands
   * as a grid item of `.hero__stage` in `index.astro` rather than inside a
   * box of this component's; the ledger does not land here, see
   * `LedgerPortal` in `HeroPlayerIsland.tsx`.
   *
   * `HeroPlayerIsland.tsx` carries why one component renders both. In short,
   * `Player.Root` renders no DOM, so one controller can stand above two panels
   * and the ledger has no second one to be reading. */
}
<HeroPlayerIsland
  client:only="react"
  captionsSrc={captionsUrl}
  source={clipUrl}
/>
{
  /* With JavaScript the island above is the whole hero and this is inert text
   * the parser never fetches from. Without it, this is the whole player: the
   * browser's own controls over the same file, and `preload="none"` so the page
   * keeps the property it argues from — nothing is fetched until the reader
   * asks for it. `muted` for the reason the island passes `defaultMuted`.
   *
   * A real `<video>` rather than a paragraph explaining what is missing,
   * because the clip is a static file on this origin and the platform already
   * ships a player for it.
   *
   * It repeats the bezel and the stage so that the fallback is a switched-off
   * instrument rather than a bare video in a gap, and it does NOT repeat the
   * ledger or the caption above it. There is no Playdeck player on this page
   * without JavaScript, so there is nothing for a ledger to report and the
   * caption's sentence about `loading="interaction"` would be describing
   * something that is not running. The caption here says what is true instead.
   *
   * `<noscript>` is `display: none` while scripts run, so this is not a grid
   * item beside the island — it is the hero's only item exactly when the island
   * is not there. */
}
<noscript>
  <figure class="demo">
    <div class="demo__bezel">
      <div class="demo__stage">
        <video
          class="demo__fallback"
          controls
          muted
          playsinline
          preload="none"
          src={clipUrl}></video>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figcaption class="demo__caption">
      The same clip in the browser's own player, because this page's player is a
      React component and scripts are off. Nothing is fetched until the controls
      are used.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</noscript>

<style>
  /* The player panel, and one of the hero row's two grid items. `min-inline-size`
   * because a grid item's automatic minimum is its content and the content here
   * is a picture: without it the clip's intrinsic width pushes the column wider
   * than its track. */
  :global(.demo) {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    min-inline-size: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* The raised body of the instrument. It carries `--elevation-panel` and no
   * border at all: `DESIGN.md` rule 4 as amended allows an elevated element
   * exactly one of the two, and border-under-blur is the pairing that stays
   * banned. `--space-3` all round is what makes it read as a body holding a
   * screen rather than as a card with a picture in it. */
  /* Always dark (#542 phase 2: "the stage is always dark" — a cinema is dark
   * regardless of the room). `--stage-*` never moves with `data-theme`, so
   * this panel and the ledger below it stop following the site's own theme
   * switch and read the same in both — a deliberate exception the section
   * comment above named as one thing this phase adds rather than something
   * left over from rule 4. */
  :global(.demo__bezel) {
    display: grid;
    padding: var(--space-3);
    background-color: var(--stage-surface);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    box-shadow: 0 8px 16px var(--dark-shadow-deep);
  }

  /* The screen, and the reason a dormant panel is worth looking at.
   *
   * Nothing is loaded until the affordance is pressed, so there is no frame to
   * show and this component may not invent one — no poster, no still, no
   * gradient standing in for a picture. What it can do instead is make the
   * emptiness read as a switched-off instrument rather than as a missing image.
   *
   * The ratio is the library's own documented fallback and is also the ratio of
   * `public/tracer.mp4`, so the box is the right shape before a frame is
   * decoded and does not resize when one arrives. The background is stated here
   * as well as passed to the player as `--playdeck-color-backdrop`, so the box
   * is already the screen at first paint rather than a hole showing the page
   * through it.
   *
   * A recessed colour and a hairline, which is the depth treatment for a box
   * whose fill sits a step or two from its ground. Nothing here is elevated —
   * the bezel around it is. */
  :global(.demo__stage) {
    display: grid;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    overflow: hidden;
    background-color: var(--stage-sunken);
    border: var(--line-width) solid var(--stage-line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);

    /* ---- the player's tokens, spoken in this site's roles ----------------
     *
     * Set here rather than on the viewport because the theme reads each one
     * with `var(--name, default)` and never declares it, so a value on any
     * ancestor reaches every part. One element to read, and adding a control
     * later needs nothing added here.
     */

    /* The well behind the media, which is what shows before a frame is
     * decoded. `--color-sunken` is the recessed-surface role — the same one
     * the code block on this page sits in. */
    --playdeck-color-backdrop: var(--stage-sunken);
    /* The control bar and the activation button share this pair. A raised
     * panel in the stage's own dark surface rather than the theme's
     * translucent black — the stage is always dark now, so this token no
     * longer follows `data-theme`; it is one of the two things #542 phase 2
     * pins outright. `--stage-ink` on `--stage-surface` is the same contrast
     * pair `DESIGN.md`'s dark table already records. */
    --playdeck-color-surface: var(--stage-surface);
    --playdeck-color-on-surface: var(--stage-ink);
    /* `--playdeck-overlay-scrim` is the control bar's background, and its
     * default is a gradient. This system has exactly one gradient and
     * `Sweep.astro` draws it, so the bar takes the flat surface the rest of
     * the panel is built from. */
    --playdeck-overlay-scrim: var(--stage-surface);
    --playdeck-control-hover: var(--stage-sunken);
    --playdeck-color-accent: var(--stage-accent);
    /* One focus treatment for the whole site, and this is the belt to
     * `base.css`'s braces. That file rings every `:focus-visible` element in
     * the accent, unlayered, so it is what actually draws the ring on a control
     * in here. The theme carries a rule of its own that would draw a white one
     * if it ever did the drawing, and its default is the only `--playdeck-*`
     * value that could put a colour on this page from outside the system. */
    --playdeck-color-focus: var(--stage-accent);
    /* The seek slider's three grounds, and the one place on this panel where
     * the choice is arithmetic rather than taste.
     *
     * The track is `--color-sunken`, which is the recessed-well role and names
     * a switch track outright. The loaded range then has to be visible on it
     * and the thumb's ring has to be visible on both, which is what picks the
     * other two: on `--color-line`, the quieter separator role, the loaded
     * range measures below the 3:1 that non-text UI owes, and on the sunken
     * well it clears it. The ratios are in `DESIGN.md`.
     *
     * The thumb takes a ring because its fill cannot carry the boundary on its
     * own — the accent does not clear 3:1 against both the track and the loaded
     * range, which is the library's own finding and the reason its theme draws
     * a ring at all. `--color-ink` is what clears both. */
    --playdeck-color-track: var(--stage-sunken);
    --playdeck-color-buffered: var(--stage-line-strong);
    --playdeck-color-thumb-ring: var(--stage-ink);
    /* The time readout is machine output, so it is mono like every other
     * value on this site, at the caption rung — above the 11px floor. */
    --playdeck-font-family: var(--font-mono);
    --playdeck-font-size: var(--text-sm);
    --playdeck-font-size-small: var(--text-xs);
    --playdeck-space-1: var(--space-1);
    --playdeck-space-2: var(--space-2);
    --playdeck-space-3: var(--space-3);
    --playdeck-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    --playdeck-radius-large: var(--radius-md);
    --playdeck-control-size: var(--hit-target);
    --playdeck-transition-duration: var(--duration-fast);
    --playdeck-transition-easing: var(--ease);
  }

  /* One cell, and every layer in it.
   *
   * Where the layers sit is the page's to decide and not the theme's: the
   * library's stylesheet states appearance and leaves geometry out, so that a
   * player works with no stylesheet at all and so that a consumer can put the
   * controls somewhere other than over the picture. The media states one thing
   * about its own size — that it fills the box it is laid into — and everything
   * else about this arrangement is the three rules below.
   *
   * A single named area rather than absolute positioning, because that is what
   * makes the picture the thing that sizes the cell: the overlays are laid into
   * the same cell without taking it out of flow, so nothing here has to restate
   * a height the media already determines.
   *
   * The ratio is read from the property the library writes onto this element
   * once a provider has measured its media, with the same fallback as the stage
   * above. So the box is right before anything has loaded and stays right if
   * the media turns out not to be the shape the fallback assumed. */
  :global(.demo__stage) :global([data-playdeck-part='viewport']) {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-areas: 'stack';
    aspect-ratio: var(--playdeck-media-aspect-ratio, 16 / 9);
    inline-size: 100%;
  }

  :global(.demo__stage) :global([data-playdeck-part='media']) {
    grid-area: stack;
    /* A grid item's automatic minimum is its content, and a video's content is
     * its intrinsic width. Without this the picture would refuse to shrink
     * below that and push the panel wider than its column. */
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }

  /* The activation affordance places itself: it comes out of the library
   * already taken out of flow and centred in whatever box it is given, so this
   * only has to say which box that is.
   *
   * Deliberately not restated here as a `place-self`. The library sets that
   * geometry inline, inline styles beat any stylesheet, and a rule that cannot
   * take effect is worse than no rule — it reads as the thing holding the
   * button in the middle of the picture.
   *
   * ---- why the button is the whole screen -----------------------------------
   *
   * A click anywhere on the picture has to start the clip, and the way to get
   * that is to stop shrinking the control rather than to add a second one. The
   * library ships this part `position: absolute; inset: 0` with auto margins,
   * which is full-bleed on its own and centres itself only once a stylesheet
   * gives it a fixed size — the bundled theme's 4rem is what was doing that.
   * Undoing the size here is therefore a return to the library's default, and
   * it costs no handler, no wrapper and no second tab stop: the one `<button>`
   * that was already the affordance is now the size of the thing people aim at.
   *
   * The badge is what the button used to be, redrawn as a background so the
   * picture is not painted over. `--space-8` is 4rem, so the face is the size
   * the theme drew it and the only thing that moved is the target.
   *
   * `border-radius` has to come back with the size, and it is the half of this
   * that a rect measurement cannot see. The theme rounds this part to 50%,
   * which on a 4rem square is the badge's circle and on a full-bleed box is an
   * ellipse inscribed in the stage — and a rounded corner clips hit testing,
   * not just paint. So the element's box was the whole picture while the
   * region that answered a click was the ellipse inside it: the four corners
   * and the whole top and bottom band were dead, and `getBoundingClientRect`
   * reported full-bleed throughout. Measured by probing `elementFromPoint` on
   * a grid rather than by clicking the middle, which is the one point that
   * could never have caught it. Squared off here, and the badge below carries
   * its own roundness, so the two are no longer one declaration.
   *
   * The ring goes inside the box, not outside it. `base.css` sets
   * `outline-offset: 2px` for the whole site, which is right for a control with
   * room around it and wrong for one flush with the edge of a stage that clips
   * its overflow — at +2px the ring is drawn where nothing is painted. Negated
   * rather than zeroed, so there is still a hairline of screen between the
   * stage's border and the ring.
   *
   * `place-content` is the half of the centring the theme does not do. Its
   * `place-items: center` centres the badge inside its grid area, and the area
   * is one auto-sized row that a full-height button leaves standing at the top
   * — `start` being what `align-content` defaults to. Both are needed. */
  :global(.demo__stage) :global([data-playdeck-part='activation']) {
    grid-area: stack;
    grid-template-areas: 'face';
    place-content: center;
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: 100%;
    background-color: transparent;
    border-radius: 0;
    outline-offset: calc(-1 * var(--space-1));
  }

  :global(.demo__stage) :global([data-playdeck-part='activation'])::before {
    content: '';
    grid-area: face;
    inline-size: var(--space-8);
    block-size: var(--space-8);
    background-color: var(--stage-surface);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    transition: transform var(--duration-fast) var(--ease);
  }

  /* The affordance answers the pointer: the badge grows a step under the
   * cursor. `transform` only — the one property pair this system animates —
   * and the site-wide reduced-motion collapse lands it instantly on either
   * settled scale. */
  :global(.demo__stage) :global([data-playdeck-part='activation']:hover)::before {
    transform: scale(1.08);
  }

  /* The icon shares the badge's cell rather than following it in a second row,
   * and paints over it because it comes later. The theme's `place-items:
   * center` is what centres each of the two inside that one cell.
   *
   * The child combinator is inside `:global()` and not outside it, which is the
   * difference between this rule working and this rule doing nothing. Astro
   * scopes every compound it is not told to leave alone, so `:global(a) > svg`
   * asks for an `svg` carrying this component's scope attribute — and the icon
   * comes out of the island, where no scope attribute reaches. Written that way
   * first, and caught by the badge and the glyph rendering as two stacked rows.
   */
  :global(.demo__stage) :global([data-playdeck-part='activation'] > svg) {
    grid-area: face;
  }

  /* Once the clip is running the same gesture toggles it, and the element that
   * answers is `Player.PlayButton` with its box opened out — see the rationale
   * in `HeroPlayerIsland.tsx`. Everything below undoes the theme's control-bar
   * treatment of that part for this one instance: the bar's buttons are 44px
   * chips with a hover tint, and a hover tint at this size would wash the
   * picture.
   *
   * `position` and `z-index` for the reason the control bar carries them — the
   * media declares a layer of its own and an unpositioned box in the same cell
   * paints underneath it. The bar takes the same `z-index` and comes later in
   * the markup, so it wins the overlap and its own buttons keep their clicks.
   *
   * No focus rule: it is not in the tab order, and a pointer press on a
   * `:focus-visible` site leaves no ring. */
  :global(.demo__stage) :global([data-surface-toggle]) {
    grid-area: stack;
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: 100%;
    border-radius: 0;
  }

  :global(.demo__stage) :global([data-surface-toggle]:hover) {
    background-color: transparent;
  }

  /* Pinned to the foot of the picture, and separated from it by the same
   * hairline that frames the panel — the bar is opaque, so without one its edge
   * would be the only boundary on this page drawn by a colour change alone.
   *
   * `position` and `z-index` are what put it in front of the picture rather
   * than behind it. The media declares a layer of its own, so a box left
   * unpositioned in the same cell paints underneath it however late it comes in
   * the markup — measured, by watching the bar disappear when the first frame
   * arrived. Stacking here is asked for, not inherited from source order. */
  :global(.demo__stage) :global([data-playdeck-part='controls']) {
    grid-area: stack;
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    place-self: end stretch;
    border-block-start: var(--line-width) solid var(--stage-line);
  }

  /* The `[hidden]` reset, needed here rather than assumed: the attribute is
   * honoured by a rule in the user-agent stylesheet, and the theme gives this
   * part a `display` of its own, which any author rule beats. So the bar the
   * island marks hidden before a player exists stays on screen unless this says
   * otherwise. Unlayered and unqualified, which is enough — the theme's own
   * declaration sits inside a cascade layer and loses to this one whatever the
   * two specificities are. */
  :global(.demo__stage) :global([data-playdeck-part='controls'][hidden]) {
    display: none;
  }

  /* The seek input, taken out of the text line it was sitting in.
   *
   * A range input is inline-level, so in `seek-slider` it is laid into a line
   * box and the container grows past it by the descender space under the
   * baseline. The theme then centres the track on the CONTAINER
   * (`inset-block-start: 50%` on `seek-buffered`) while the input keeps the top
   * of the line box, so the thumb and the bar it runs along do not share a
   * centre. How far apart is a function of the consumer's font, so every
   * consumer of the bundled theme has some version of the offset and this
   * panel — whose `--playdeck-font-size` is the site's mono at `--text-sm` —
   * had its own.
   *
   * `display: block` removes the line box, so the container is the input's own
   * 44px and the 50% the theme centres on is the input's centre. It is the
   * whole fix, and it is a fix in the right place only for this panel — the
   * defect is `theme.css`'s and reaches every consumer of the bundled theme,
   * where it is reported rather than patched here (#520 puts `packages/` out of
   * this work's scope, and `e2e/thumb-contrast.spec.ts` pins that geometry).
   *
   * The 44px target is untouched: this changes the input's outer display type
   * and none of its box. */
  :global(.demo__stage) :global([data-playdeck-part='seek-slider-input']) {
    display: block;
  }

  /* Plex Mono is set tight relative to its own width, and this is the site's
   * rule for opening it wherever it appears. The theme sets the family from the
   * token above but has no tracking of its own to set. */
  :global(.demo__stage) :global([data-playdeck-part='time']) {
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-fn);
  }

  /* The fallback fills its stage rather than sizing itself from the clip, so
   * the no-JavaScript panel is the same box the player's is. */
  :global(.demo__fallback) {
    inline-size: 100%;
    block-size: 100%;
  }

  :global(.demo__caption) {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--stage-ink-subtle);
  }

  /* ---- the capability ledger ----------------------------------------------
   *
   * The most on-thesis element on the page: the product's claim is that what a
   * player reports about itself is true, and this is a player's report. It used
   * to be a full-width strip of white with three rows adrift in it and the
   * reason column flung against the far edge — a faint table, which is the one
   * thing the page's signature could not afford to be.
   *
   * It is built the way the player above it is: a raised body holding a
   * recessed readout. Same two boxes, same two roles, and the pairing is what
   * makes them read as a matched pair of instruments rather than as two
   * unrelated panels that happen to share a row.
   *
   * These rules used to live in `index.astro`, beside the sample data they
   * styled. They are here because the markup is: the panel is rendered by the
   * island this component mounts, so its look belongs to the component that
   * decides how the island looks. */
  :global(.ledger) {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    min-inline-size: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* The one element on the site that takes `--elevation-instrument`, which is
   * the deeper of the two steps and exists for exactly this: the panel a page
   * is built around. `DESIGN.md` rule 4 names it here by name.
   *
   * No border, which is not a stylistic preference but the whole of the
   * amended rule — a hairline under a blur is the tell that stays banned, and
   * an elevated box drawing one is how it would come back. Colour alone
   * already raises this panel (`--color-surface` on `--color-field` is built
   * for it); the elevation is what makes it the thing the eye lands on. */
  :global(.ledger__panel) {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    padding: var(--space-4);
    background-color: var(--stage-surface);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    box-shadow: 0 8px 16px var(--dark-shadow-deep);
  }

  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    :global(.ledger__panel) {
      padding: var(--space-5);
    }
  }

  /* An `h2` set at the caption rung rather than a styled `div`: the panel is a
   * named region of the page, so it keeps a heading a reader can navigate to,
   * and what it does not keep is `h2`'s default size — this is the label on an
   * instrument, not a section title competing with the ones below. In the mono,
   * because everything else inside this panel is machine output and a label in
   * the prose face would be the one part of the readout written by the page. */
  :global(.ledger__title) {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: var(--weight-regular);
    color: var(--stage-ink-subtle);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-fn);
  }

  /* The panel's own state, said in the machine register the rest of the
   * readout speaks. It is the line that makes the dormant panel read as a
   * before rather than a failure — five ambers under a line that says
   * "dormant · press play" are a report waiting to be asked for. At the
   * bottom rung, like the reason lines: it qualifies the readout, it is not
   * part of it. */
  :global(.ledger__state) {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--stage-ink-subtle);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-fn);
  }

  /* Once a provider has attached, the state line is a fact about a live
   * player rather than an instruction, and it takes the full ink the rows'
   * answers take. A colour and not a motion, so there is nothing here for
   * reduced motion to have an opinion about. */
  :global(.ledger__state[data-live]) {
    color: var(--stage-ink);
  }

  /* The readout. A recessed well inside the raised body, which is the same
   * pair of roles the player's screen and bezel use — `--color-sunken` is what
   * `tokens.css` calls a recessed well, and rows sitting in one read as output
   * rather than as a list somebody typed.
   *
   * `--space-4` between rows rather than `--space-3`, because a row is two
   * lines whenever it carries a reason and the gap has to be larger than the
   * gap inside a row for the five to separate at all.
   *
   * The list reset is written here rather than shared with the lists in
   * `index.astro`, for the reason at the head of this block: a rule in that
   * file cannot reach markup the island renders. */
  :global(.ledger__rows) {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    padding: var(--space-4);
    margin: 0;
    background-color: var(--stage-sunken);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* Below 24rem a row wraps, because the four parts are of very different
   * lengths and there is no width to align them in. */
  :global(.row) {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-1) var(--space-3);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  /* An entry, not a table row: what is being asked sits on the first line with
   * the answer pushed to the far end of it, and what qualifies the answer sits
   * under both. Two lines rather than four columns because the reason is the
   * one part with no fixed length, and giving it a column of its own is what
   * used to strand it against the far edge of a full-width panel with the
   * question it belongs to a hand's width away.
   *
   * The status words still sit under one another, which is the whole point of
   * printing five of them: a reader compares `available` with `unknown` by
   * looking down. They align to the end of the row rather than to a column of
   * fixed width, so nothing has to guess how long the longest one is. */
  @media (min-width: 24rem) {
    :global(.row) {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
      column-gap: var(--space-3);
      row-gap: var(--space-1);
    }

    :global(.row__dot) {
      grid-row: 1;
    }

    :global(.row__reason) {
      grid-column: 2 / -1;
    }
  }

  /* The state dot. The same shape and the same `--status-color` the three-state
   * comparison on the landing page marks a status with, drawn here because
   * these rows are the island's and a rule in that page cannot reach them.
   *
   * `--radius-pill` is the token that means "fully round" here; `50%` would be
   * a second spelling of it. The 8px is a one-off — a shape's own size, not a
   * step of the spacing scale — so it is written here rather than promoted to a
   * token nothing else would read. */
  :global(.row__dot) {
    align-self: center;
    flex: none;
    inline-size: 0.5rem;
    block-size: 0.5rem;
    background-color: var(--status-color);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
  }

  /* The capability is the subject of its row and takes the body rung in the
   * mono where the panel has the width for it — a readout whose largest text
   * is the same size as its captions is what made this panel read as faint. */
  /* `color` stated explicitly rather than inherited: the panel is pinned dark
   * regardless of the page's own theme (`--stage-*` never follows
   * `data-theme`), and without this the capability name inherited
   * `--color-ink` instead — dark ink on a dark well, invisible in the light
   * theme. Caught by looking at a light-theme screenshot; every other line in
   * this panel already states its own `--stage-*` colour. */
  :global(.row__capability) {
    min-inline-size: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--stage-ink);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-fn);
  }

  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    :global(.row__capability) {
      font-size: var(--text-md);
    }
  }

  /* The answer, in the colour its status selects. `--status-color` is assigned
   * by the `[data-status]` rules in `base.css`, and it has to be a shared
   * stylesheet rather than either component: Astro scopes a component's styles
   * to the markup that component compiled, and these rows come out of the
   * island, so a rule written here or in `index.astro` would match nothing
   * they render. It was written in `index.astro` first, and the whole ledger
   * lost its colour — the variable resolved to nothing, so the word went plain
   * and the dot painted transparent.
   *
   * The colour does not transition. A row's answer changes the moment the
   * controller's does, which is the behaviour this panel exists to show, and a
   * colour is not one of the two properties this system animates (`DESIGN.md`
   * rule 5) — it snaps. The settle the rows do take when the panel goes live
   * is the resolution animation at the foot of this file, which moves
   * `opacity` and `transform` over content that is already true and never
   * delays an answer arriving in the document. */
  :global(.row__status) {
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    color: var(--status-color);
  }

  /* The reason line: the smallest text on the site, and the text carrying the
   * product's whole argument. `--text-fn` is the bottom rung and there is
   * nothing below it, in rem so a reader who raised their root size gets a
   * larger floor rather than the same one.
   *
   * It sits under the entry it qualifies and starts where that entry starts,
   * so a reader reads down rather than across: `unknown` and `not-ready` are
   * one answer in two parts, and a panel that separated them by the width of
   * the page would be making them look like two facts. */
  :global(.row__reason) {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--stage-ink-subtle);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-fn);
  }

  :global(.ledger__caption) {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--stage-ink-subtle);
  }

  /* ---- the resolution, made visible ---------------------------------------
   *
   * The third of the three animations this app writes, and the one spent on
   * the page's whole point: the moment a provider attaches, the five rows
   * stop being "nothing has been asked" and become that provider's own
   * report, and this is what marks the swap so a reader sees it happen
   * rather than blinking and missing it.
   *
   * It is keyed off `data-live`, which `HeroPlayerIsland.tsx` writes only
   * when `provider !== null` — a fact read from the controller, in the same
   * React commit that writes the rows' new answers into the DOM. Nothing
   * here is simulated: the animation moves `opacity` and `transform` over
   * content that is already true, it never delays the truth arriving in the
   * document, and it runs once, when the attribute appears.
   *
   * The rows land in sequence, top to bottom, which is what makes the panel
   * read as an instrument printing its answers instead of five things
   * flickering at once. The delays are steps of `--duration-fast` rather
   * than literals, per rule 1.
   *
   * Under `prefers-reduced-motion` the animation is removed outright rather
   * than left to the site-wide duration collapse: that collapse shortens
   * durations but not delays, and a delayed `both`-filled row would sit
   * invisible through its delay — a row hidden from a reader who asked for
   * less motion, not less truth. `animation: none` lands every row on its
   * settled state, which is the CSS's own resting state. */
  :global(.ledger__rows[data-live] .row) {
    animation: ledger-resolve var(--duration-base) var(--ease) both;
  }

  :global(.ledger__rows[data-live] .row:nth-child(2)) {
    animation-delay: var(--duration-fast);
  }

  :global(.ledger__rows[data-live] .row:nth-child(3)) {
    animation-delay: calc(var(--duration-fast) * 2);
  }

  :global(.ledger__rows[data-live] .row:nth-child(4)) {
    animation-delay: calc(var(--duration-fast) * 3);
  }

  :global(.ledger__rows[data-live] .row:nth-child(5)) {
    animation-delay: calc(var(--duration-fast) * 4);
  }

  @keyframes ledger-resolve {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(var(--space-1));
    }

    to {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: translateY(0);
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    :global(.ledger__rows[data-live] .row) {
      animation: none;
    }
  }

  /* ---- the stage's own status colour --------------------------------------
   *
   * `base.css`'s `[data-status]` rules point `--status-color` at
   * `--color-available`/`--color-unknown`/`--color-unavailable`, which follow
   * `data-theme`. The ledger now sits on the stage, which does not, so its
   * rows are re-pointed here at the pinned dark-theme values — already
   * measured >=6.8:1 on this ground. Higher specificity than `base.css`'s
   * unscoped rules (two compounds against one) and unlayered like them, so it
   * wins without an `!important` fight. */
  :global(.ledger) :global([data-status='available']) {
    --status-color: var(--stage-available);
  }

  :global(.ledger) :global([data-status='unknown']) {
    --status-color: var(--stage-unknown);
  }

  :global(.ledger) :global([data-status='unavailable']) {
    --status-color: var(--stage-unavailable);
  }

  /* ---- CH 01, the assembly ------------------------------------------------
   *
   * The signature moment: as the reader scrolls through the 300vh section
   * `index.astro` builds around this pinned player, `index.astro`'s own
   * script writes `data-assembly-stage="0".."5"` onto that section — one
   * integer, advanced by five sentinels, never a continuous scroll-linked
   * value (the plan's own "discrete states, not continuous scroll-linking").
   *
   * **The resting state — no attribute at all — is every part shown.** That is
   * what a page with no script, or a reader who asked for reduced motion (in
   * which case `index.astro` never constructs the observer), gets: the
   * from-state is added here by attribute value and nothing is hidden by
   * default, the same rule `base.css`'s `.u-enter` and this page's own
   * `.tour-step` already hold to.
   *
   * The five stages gate the control bar's own parts — real ones, the same
   * elements `ControlBar` in `HeroPlayerIsland.tsx` always rendered, never a
   * second illustrative copy — plus the caption overlay, which lives in the
   * viewport rather than the bar. They only have anything to reveal once the
   * bar itself is showing, i.e. once the reader has pressed play — before
   * that, `hidden={!ready}` already keeps the whole bar out of the document,
   * and this page's own copy invites the press before the assembly starts. */
  :global([data-assembly-stage='0'] [data-playdeck-part='play-button']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage='0'] [data-playdeck-part='mute-button']) {
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  :global([data-assembly-stage='0'] [data-playdeck-part='seek-slider']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage='0'] [data-playdeck-part='time']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage='1'] [data-playdeck-part='seek-slider']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage='1'] [data-playdeck-part='time']) {
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  :global([data-assembly-stage='0'] [data-playdeck-part='captions']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage='1'] [data-playdeck-part='captions']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage='2'] [data-playdeck-part='captions']) {
    opacity: 0;
  }

  :global([data-assembly-stage='0'] [data-playdeck-part='captions-menu']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage='1'] [data-playdeck-part='captions-menu']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage='2'] [data-playdeck-part='captions-menu']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage='3'] [data-playdeck-part='captions-menu']) {
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  :global([data-assembly-stage='0'] [data-playdeck-part='fullscreen-button']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage='1'] [data-playdeck-part='fullscreen-button']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage='2'] [data-playdeck-part='fullscreen-button']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage='3'] [data-playdeck-part='fullscreen-button']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage='4'] [data-playdeck-part='fullscreen-button']) {
    opacity: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  :global([data-assembly-stage] [data-playdeck-part='play-button']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage] [data-playdeck-part='mute-button']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage] [data-playdeck-part='seek-slider']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage] [data-playdeck-part='time']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage] [data-playdeck-part='captions']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage] [data-playdeck-part='captions-menu']),
  :global([data-assembly-stage] [data-playdeck-part='fullscreen-button']) {
    transition: opacity var(--duration-slow) var(--ease);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    :global([data-assembly-stage] [data-playdeck-part]) {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
</style>
